 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The merging step itself is $O(K)$ per cluster for moment calculation and $O(K)$ for amplitude refit (assuming constant-time overlap evaluation). Partitioning dominates the preprocessing cost. Greedy hierarchical partitioning is generally $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N^2)$ depending on implementation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the cluster members and intermediate moment sums. No significant additional memory overhead compared to standard merging [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Cost-increment relocation matches greedy merging performance within 5.5% at compression factors of four and above [S1, unofficial]. On full fits, cost-aware merging outperforms mass-ranked culling regardless of amplitude refit [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides a theoretically grounded optimal amplitude that minimizes L2 error, leading to better reconstruction quality on dense data compared to heuristic merging [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** More computationally intensive than simple averaging due to the overlap integral calculations. Less effective than culling on sparse subsamples [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The merging step is $O(K)$ for moment calculation and $O(1)$ for the analytical L2 optimization (for $K=2$) or $O(K)$ for the weighted average approximation. Partitioning dominates the cost, typically $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N^2)$ depending on the algorithm (greedy vs. relocation).
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the input splats and $O(M)$ for the output, where $M$ is the number of clusters.
+*   **Performance:** Cost-increment relocation matches greedy merging performance within 5.5% at compression factors of four and above [S1, unofficial]. On full fits, cost-aware merging outperforms mass-ranked culling regardless of amplitude refit; on sparse subsamples, culling wins in the median [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Preserves spatial extent and mass better than simple averaging; provides a theoretical bound on error via the L2 metric.
+*   **Limitations:** The analytical L2 solution is complex for $K > 2$; requires careful handling of covariance matrix positive-definiteness.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the Luxar project, specifically from supplementary documents detailing substitutive levels of detail for Gaussian splats [S1, unofficial]. It is part of a broader effort to create scalable interactive web visualisation of multidimensional scientific data, particularly microscopy data [S1, unofficial]. The technique is described in the context of a cost-metric design space including L2, Cauchy–Schwarz, gKL, W2, and MMD metrics [S1, unofficial].
+The method originates from the Luxar project, which focuses on Gaussian splatting for microscopy and scalable interactive web visualization of multidimensional scientific data [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the need for efficient Level-of-Detail (LOD) generation in 3D Gaussian Splatting, specifically for substitutive LOD where splats are replaced by merged representatives rather than culled [S1, unofficial]. The theoretical underpinnings include the analytical merging error for Gaussian splats and the design space for substitutive-LOD cost metrics (L2, Cauchy-Schwarz, gKL, W2, MMD, L∞) [S1, unofficial].
 
